  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Same direction: higher prices bring forth more output. The opposite relationship is the law of demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Producers respond to price. The first option is the demand law and the last is a separate claim about costs.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Willing and able, at each price, over a period — the same structure as the definition of demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The good's own price. The other three are determinants, so they shift the curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Left: at every price the firm now offers less, because each unit costs more to make.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Right: lower unit cost means more can be supplied at every price.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A subsidy is a negative cost, so supply shifts right and the price paid by buyers falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    More sellers mean more offered at every price. The price effect is a fall, not a rise.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The quantities are equal. *Demand equals supply* is loose language that gives the right answer only when it is read as a quantity statement.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A surplus pushes the price down. Sellers with unsold stock cut prices until the excess is cleared.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A shortage pulls the price up: buyers who cannot get the good bid against each other.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Demand exceeds supply by 40 at that price, which is a shortage and will push the price up.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Supply exceeds demand by 25, an unsold surplus that forces the price down.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A rightward demand shift along a fixed supply curve gives a higher price and a larger quantity.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A rightward supply shift lowers the price and increases the quantity traded.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A leftward demand shift lowers both price and quantity.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A leftward supply shift raises the price and reduces the quantity traded.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Below equilibrium, demand exceeds supply: the ceiling is binding and the shortage appears at once.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Above equilibrium, supply exceeds demand and the surplus must be absorbed, often by the state.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A binding ceiling creates a shortage, and buyers who cannot get the good legally will pay more than the ceiling illegally.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A tax is a cost, so supply shifts left; the price buyers pay rises and the price sellers keep falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A subsidy shifts supply right, lowering the price and raising the quantity traded.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A price transmits information about scarcity, and the responses of buyers and sellers coordinate without any planner.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Neither surplus nor shortage, so there is no pressure on the price to move.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Both shifts push quantity up. Price, by contrast, could move either way, which is the question that follows.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Both changes push the price up, so the price is certain even though the quantity is not.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Above equilibrium, so that the market price cannot fall to its natural level. Set below, the floor never binds.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A drought reduces output at every price. A good harvest shifts supply right, a price fall is a movement, and income affects demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The long run allows capacity to be built and inputs reorganised, which is what makes supply responsive.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Buyers bid the price up, which both rations the existing supply and calls forth more of it.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The price effects cancel and the quantity effects reinforce, so quantity rises and price stays where it was.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The good's own price moves the firm along the supply curve; it does not shift it. The other three are determinants.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    100 − 2P = 40 + 2P gives 60 = 4P, so P = ₦15 and Q = 70. Substituting back checks both equations.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    At P = ₦15, Qd = 100 − 30 = 70 and Qs = 40 + 30 = 70, so the equilibrium quantity is 70 units.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Sellers cut the price to clear unsold stock, which raises quantity demanded and reduces quantity supplied until they match.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Ability and willingness to pay. The other three describe rationing by queue, by coupon or by need, each of which requires a non-price rule.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    No pressure on the price. Buyers without the ability to pay are still excluded, which is why the first option is wrong.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The quantity is fixed once the goods arrive, so it is offered at whatever price it will fetch — a vertical supply curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Buyers switch towards X, so demand for X rises at every price. The substitute's price is a determinant of X's demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Quantity falls because both shifts push it down; price could move either way depending on which shift is larger.
  ]

]

